/* Layered on top of qdarktheme, not instead of it.
 *
 * NO COLOURS IN THIS FILE. qdarktheme paints with literals in its own
 * stylesheet and installs a placeholder palette, so both palette(...) roles and
 * its own template placeholders are dead here — see the note in __init__.py,
 * which has the two reasons. Our widgets are described in terms qdarktheme
 * already colours (QPushButton:default, QFrame[frameShape=Panel]) and this file
 * adjusts nothing but geometry. Nothing here needs to follow the OS theme,
 * because nothing here knows what colour it is.
 *
 * The numbers are theme.SPACING (8) and theme.RADIUS (8) and their multiples.
 * Qt stylesheets have no variables, so those are kept in step by hand; they
 * live in one file each.
 */

QPushButton#primary,
QPushButton#secondary {
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    min-height: 16px;
}

/* Colour, hover, pressed and disabled all come from qdarktheme's
 * QPushButton:default rules, which primary_button() opts into. */
QPushButton#primary { font-weight: 600; }

QFrame#card {
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 0;
}

/* No blanket rule for the card's children. `QFrame#card > QWidget` outranks
 * `QPushButton:default` on specificity, so clearing their background and border
 * "for tidiness" erased the accent fill on every primary button inside a card
 * and the outline on every secondary one. qdarktheme already makes labels
 * transparent (QLabel:!window), which was the only thing that rule fixed. */

QLabel#heading1 { font-size: 16pt; font-weight: 600; }
QLabel#heading2 { font-size: 12pt; font-weight: 600; }
QLabel#heading3 { font-size: 10pt; font-weight: 600; }
